      "question": "Did Qualcomm's customer-concentration risk change from FY2024 to FY2025, or is it mostly the same warning? Give me a concise risk brief.",
      "answer": "## Risk Brief: Qualcomm Customer-Concentration Disclosure, FY2024 10-K vs FY2025 10-K\n\n**Periods compared:** FY2024 10-K (fiscal year ended September 29, 2024; filed November 6, 2024) [S1] vs. FY2025 10-K (fiscal year ended September 28, 2025; filed November 5, 2025) [S2].\n\n### What changed and why it matters\n\n**1. New severity language on Apple modem transition (material).** In the customer-concentration risk factor, both filings disclose that Apple buys Qualcomm's lower-margin MDM/thin-modem products. S1 stated only that revenues could be negatively impacted to the extent Apple takes device share from Qualcomm's integrated modem+processor customers [S1]. S2 adds an explicit expectation that \"Apple will increasingly use its own modem products, rather than our products, in its future devices, which will have a significant negative impact on our QCT revenues, results of operations and cash flows\" [S2]. This is a new, directly quantified severity statement introducing a specific QCT segment impact that was not in the prior year's disclosure [S1][S2].\n\n**2. Broader named counterparties in vertical integration (material).** S1 named \"Samsung\" as the sole example of a largest customer developing its own integrated circuits, with Apple's in-house modem program described separately [S1]. S2 names \"Apple, Samsung and Xiaomi\" as developing their own integrated circuit products and states Qualcomm expects them to use those products in some or all future devices [S2]. Xiaomi is newly added as a named counterparty [S1][S2]. The underlying warning on customer vertical integration was already present in S1, so this is an expanded, changed scope of the existing concentration exposure rather than a new risk category [S1][S2].\n\n### Largely stable disclosure\nThe core concentration warning — significant revenue from a small number of customers/licensees, premium-tier handset reliance, Chinese OEM concentration, licensing revenue concentrated among a limited number of licensees, and reduced purchases causing revenue/margin harm — is substantially the same between the two filings [S1][S2]. Minor wording changes (e.g., \"more than 300 licensees\" to \"many licensees\"; \"made in China 2025\" to \"prioritized semiconductor self-sufficiency\") do not alter the economic exposure described [S1][S2].\n\n### Post-filing context (window November 6, 2025 onward)\nReporting after the FY2025 filing corroborates the risk Qualcomm disclosed: an Apple C1 in-house modem debuted in the iPhone 16e in February 2025 (an event before this filing), and later reporting indicates Apple will continue expanding in-house modem coverage, with Qualcomm's share of Apple modem volumes expected to be materially lower over subsequent launch cycles [W2][W4]. Third-party reporting attributes to Qualcomm management at a later date projections that fiscal 2027 non-handset growth is expected to replace fiscal 2026 Apple product revenue [W5], and that the December quarter would no longer be Qualcomm's revenue peak season [W3]. These are attributed, non-official, forward-looking accounts and do not change the filed classification; the filed disclosure remains a forward-looking risk statement, and the realized QCT revenue impact is not established by the filings themselves [S1][S2][W3][W5].\n\n**Bottom line:** The FY2025 10-K materially intensifies the disclosed severity of the Apple modem-transition risk (explicit significant QCT impact) and broadens named customer counterparties to include Xiaomi, while the core customer-concentration warning otherwise remains substantially unchanged.",
